# DocEditor — configuration
#
# Every variable below is read by src/config.rs. Anything this service does not
# read has been removed rather than left to suggest a feature that does not
# exist (the previous file advertised auto-save, websocket and export knobs for
# a collaborative layer that was never built, plus DOCSTORE_URL/PDF_ENGINE_URL
# for outbound calls this service does not make).

# --- Required ---------------------------------------------------------------
# 5435 and not 5433: on the dev host, 5433 is another project's container which
# answers and then rejects the `ods` role, so the failure reads like broken code.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://ods:ods-dev-2026@127.0.0.1:5435/ods

# --- HTTP -------------------------------------------------------------------
SERVER_HOST=0.0.0.0
SERVER_PORT=8087

# --- Logging ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Applied to the tracing subscriber. RUST_LOG, when set, takes precedence.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- Documents --------------------------------------------------------------
# The ceiling on a DOCUMENT BODY, as stored, in BYTES (2 MB = 2097152 bytes of
# UTF-8 — not characters: a Chinese document reaches it three times sooner than
# an ASCII one, which is what storage actually costs). The HTTP payload that
# carries one is bounded separately and higher (2x + 64 KiB, see
# src/api/payload.rs): JSON wraps the body in quotes and escapes some of its
# characters, so setting the two equal — which this file said until 2026-09-14 —
# makes a document of exactly this size impossible to send. Over the body
# ceiling is a 422 naming the limit; a request too long to read at all is a 413.
#
# 2 and not 10 since 2026-09-14, and the number is MEASURED, not preferred.
# It is one of three settings that only make sense together: this ceiling, the
# instance memory (ops/cloudrun/doceditor.json: 512Mi) and the number of
# requests admitted at once (Cloud Run's default of 80, since that file sets no
# --concurrency). N concurrent full-size saves of distinct documents, in a
# cgroup at 512 MiB:
#
#     10 MB, N=10 -> 200 x10, peak 451 MiB     10 MB, N=12 -> oom-kill, pid=0
#      4 MB, N=80 -> oom-kill, pid=0            3 MB, N=80 -> 200 x80, 475 MiB
#      2 MB, N=80 -> 200 x80, peak 340 MiB
#
# An OOM kill is not a failed request: the INSTANCE dies, so every other
# tenant's in-flight request dies with it. Settled by HR-20260914-001, option A.
# RAISING THIS IS A SIZING DECISION, not a preference — the other two terms have
# to move with it, and they live outside this repository.
#
# A value that is not a positive whole number (0, "2MB", "two") is now refused
# at startup instead of being silently replaced by the default: an operator who
# lowers a safety ceiling and is not obeyed learns nothing until an instance
# dies.
MAX_DOCUMENT_SIZE_MB=2
# It is NOT a search bound. Full-text search covers the first 250 000
# characters of a document (migration 009, ADR-006); the rest is stored, served
# and versioned as usual, it is simply not in the index. PostgreSQL refuses a
# tsvector above 1 048 575 bytes of lexemes, and indexing the whole body made a
# body of varied text above ~0.8 MB impossible to store at all. With the ceiling
# at 2 MB the two no longer meet in practice, but the index bound is what makes
# that independent of how the ceiling moves next.

# --- Events -----------------------------------------------------------------
# WHICH BUS. `pubsub` (staging and production), `redpanda`/`kafka` (a local
# broker), or `none`. Leave it unset and the historical behaviour applies: a
# broker address selects Redpanda, its absence selects no bus at all.
#
# A value this service cannot honour REFUSES THE BOOT — `pubsub` without
# GCP_PROJECT_ID, `redpanda` without REDPANDA_BROKERS, a word that is neither.
# That is deliberate and it is the strictest rule in this file: a mis-set
# ceiling eventually kills an instance and leaves a trace, whereas a mis-set bus
# produces NOTHING TO NOTICE. The producer returns Ok, the callers check it, the
# tests observe that publish was called — and this service published four months
# of CloudEvents into a no-op producer that way (ADR-002). Refusing to start is
# the only variant of "something is wrong" a silent bus can be turned into.
#
# Settled by HR-20260914-007 (option A, 2026-09-14, it@orbusdigital.com,
# "Exécutant : dev, dans le dépôt doceditor"): the code publishes with rdkafka,
# the live Cloud Run revision has carried EVENT_BUS=pubsub since May 2026, and
# no Redpanda broker exists in orbus-ods-staging. See ADR-011.
EVENT_BUS=redpanda

# --- ...when EVENT_BUS=pubsub ------------------------------------------------
# The project whose topic is published to, and the topic. Both are set by the
# live revision already; GCP_PROJECT_ID has no default because guessing a
# project is how an event ends up somewhere nobody reads.
GCP_PROJECT_ID=orbus-ods-staging
PUBSUB_TOPIC=editor-events
# Credentials: none here, and none in this repository. On Cloud Run the runtime
# service account's access token comes from the instance metadata server —
# measured 2026-09-14, runtime-cloud-run@orbus-ods-staging.iam.gserviceaccount.com
# already holds roles/pubsub.publisher, which is why option A needed no
# infrastructure change. Two optional overrides, both Google's own names, both
# read straight from the environment: PUBSUB_EMULATOR_HOST (an unauthenticated
# local emulator — host:port) and GCE_METADATA_HOST.
#
# PUBSUB_TOPIC_DLQ is set on the deployed revision and is deliberately NOT read
# here: a dead-letter topic is a property of a SUBSCRIPTION, not of a publisher.
# Nothing in this service can write to it, and a variable read but unused is how
# LOG_LEVEL spent months having no effect.

# --- ...when EVENT_BUS=redpanda (local development) --------------------------
# Leave REDPANDA_BROKERS unset ONLY to run without a broker: events are then
# dropped, and the service says so at WARN on startup.
REDPANDA_BROKERS=127.0.0.1:9092
# editor-events, and editor-events-dlq beside it, are the names spec.md §4.2
# settles (executing HR-20260913-001) and the only ones of the four candidate
# spellings that exist as provisioned resources — measured 2026-09-14 among the
# 25 topics of orbus-ods-staging. `editor.events` (this line until 2026-09-14),
# `ods.editor.events` (GTM brief) and `doceditor-events` are superseded.
# `editor` is the DOMAIN — schema `editor`, event source `/editor`, types
# `com.ods.editor.*`; `doceditor` is the name of the deployment. The name is
# independent of the transport, which is why §4.2 could settle it before
# HR-20260914-007 settled the carrier.
REDPANDA_TOPIC=editor-events

# --- Authentication ---------------------------------------------------------
# Production: the OID RS256 public key, PEM, base64-encoded.
JWT_RSA_PUBLIC_KEY_B64=__NEEDS_HUMAN__
# Development only: accept HS256 with a shared secret instead.
JWT_ALLOW_HS256=false
JWT_SECRET=
JWT_ISSUER=https://oid.staging.orbusdigital.com
JWT_AUDIENCE=
